    <!--
      DayBricks localization source catalog

      Translation files belong in sibling Android resource directories:
        res/values/strings.xml       source language (English)
        res/values-ru/strings.xml    Russian
        res/values-de/strings.xml    German

      Every translator-facing entry has a stable key and a context comment. Keep
      placeholders such as %1$d and %2$s unchanged and in a grammatically useful
      order. Use <plurals> whenever the surrounding sentence changes with a count.

      Plural translation rules:
      * Android/CLDR supports zero, one, two, few, many and other. The category
        names are grammatical labels, not formulas such as "number ends in 1".
      * Keep only the categories required by the target locale and always provide
        other. Android selects the category from the active locale and quantity.
      * English needs one and other. Russian integer counts need one (1, 21, but
        not 11), few (2-4, 22-24, but not 12-14), and many (0, 5-20, 25-30,
        and analogous values). Russian other covers decimal quantities; current
        DayBricks counters are integers, but retaining other is mandatory.
      * Other languages have different rules: Arabic can use all six categories;
        languages such as Chinese commonly use only other. Never reproduce these
        rules in Kotlin and never choose a form from the final digit manually.
      * Code must call pluralStringResource/getQuantityString with the raw integer
        both as the selector and as the %1$d formatting argument.

      Locale-derived content that is not a literal string:
      * planner_date_header_pattern controls the order of weekday, day and month.
        Weekday and month names must come from the selected app locale.
      * All clock labels and time ranges use the locale's SHORT time format, so a
        locale may choose 12-hour or 24-hour time and its own separators.
      * Numeric values must use locale-aware number formatting when the app is
        wired to these resources. Durations are whole minutes.
      * The device time zone still controls instants and DST. "UTC%1$s" is only a
        disambiguation label for a repeated local hour; the offset itself is data.
      * Calendar names, account names, event titles, template names and preset
        names are user/provider data and must never be translated.
      * To support an in-app language switch, java.time formatters must be created
        with the selected app locale (not only Locale.getDefault), and Android's
        per-app locale API must recreate the UI. Resource translation alone does
        not switch system-generated weekday, month and time text.

      Do not translate glyph-only controls (+, −, ×, ✓, ‹, ›, ⋮, ⌃, ⌄), emoji,
      the #DayBricks ownership marker, serialized enum values, test tags, URLs,
      calendar IDs or synchronization protocol fields.
    -->

